# HTTP examples for module 12-security. Run them from IntelliJ IDEA / VS Code (REST Client).
# Start the lesson first: ./mvnw -pl modules/12-security/lesson spring-boot:run
# Demo users (local development only): ada / ada-password (CUSTOMER), bob / bob-password, admin / admin-password (ADMIN)

### Public
GET http://localhost:8080/api/books

### 401 — no credentials
POST http://localhost:8080/api/books
Content-Type: application/json

{"isbn": "9780000000017", "title": "Secure by Design"}

### 403 — authenticated, but a CUSTOMER may not write
POST http://localhost:8080/api/books
Authorization: Basic ada ada-password
Content-Type: application/json

{"isbn": "9780000000017", "title": "Secure by Design"}

### 201 — an ADMIN may write
POST http://localhost:8080/api/books
Authorization: Basic admin admin-password
Content-Type: application/json

{"isbn": "9780000000017", "title": "Secure by Design"}

### Method security: ada sees her own order …
GET http://localhost:8080/api/orders/1
Authorization: Basic ada ada-password

### … but not bob's (403)
GET http://localhost:8080/api/orders/2
Authorization: Basic ada ada-password

### Get a token from our Authorization Server (client credentials)
# @name token
POST http://localhost:8080/oauth2/token
Authorization: Basic bookstore-cli dev-only-secret
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&scope=books.read+books.write

### Call the API with the token
GET http://localhost:8080/api/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token.response.body.access_token}}

### The server's public keys (to verify token signatures)
GET http://localhost:8080/oauth2/jwks
